mmendation, possibly adjust vote.\n\nOur own notes: Candidate A: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized. No info on reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, etc.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, makes nasty remarks about colleagues (we have that fact). Also can be grumpy, uncooperative.\n\nCandidate C: 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere with crew, can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\nCandidate D: responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as arrogant, relatively weak leadership skills.\n\nNow Agent 2 and 3 have both shifted to Candidate C as provisional recommendation. Agent 2 also noted concerns about D's arrogance etc. Agent 1 (us) need to respond. We can either agree with C, or challenge, or bring new evidence about A or D or B. We need to ensure we cover candidate A still, as per coverage rule. We haven't discussed A yet. So we should bring A into discussion, perhaps compare A to C, and see if A could be a better fit. Provide evidence: A anticipates dangerous situations (good for stress resilience and decision quality), sees complex connections (technical competence), excellent spatial vision (situational awareness), very good leadership qualities (crew cooperation), but sometimes not good at taking criticism (feedback orientation) and can be unorganized (reliability). So we can argue that A's strengths are strong but concerns about organization and feedback may be mitigated? We need to decide if we shift vote or stay with C.\n\nGiven that C has 100% reliability and positive crew atmosphere, but communication difficulty and egocentric. A has strong anticipation and leadership but unorganized and poor at criticism. Which is better? Both have concerns. We could argue that A's unorganized nature may affect reliability more severely than C's communication issues, especially for long-distance flights where meticulous planning is needed. So we might keep C as leading candidate, but we need to bring A into discussion to satisfy coverage.\n\nThus we can say: I agree with C as leading, but we still need to discuss A's potential, especially his anticipation and leadership, but his unorganized nature and poor receptiveness to feedback could undermine reliability and crew cooperation. So I maintain provisional vote for C, but we should explore A further.\n\nThus our public message: agree with C, bring A's facts, note concerns, maintain vote for C. Provide explicit facts: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized.\n\nWe also need to include METADATA_JSON with vote Candidate C.\n\nProceed."}
